<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Speicherstadt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1883">1883</a>–<a href="/years/1927">1927</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Speicherstadt is a warehouse district in the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, built from 1883 after the city agreed to join the German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/customs-union">customs union</a> while keeping a free port. Its red-brick Gothic Revival warehouses, standing on oak piles between canals, form the largest such complex in the world, and about 20,000 people were moved to clear the site. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kontorhaus-district">Kontorhaus District</a> it became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2015.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/speicherstadt.jpg" alt="Speicherstadt" width="320" height="191" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Wolf, www.foto-tw.de · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Speicherstadt_abends.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>From 1815 the independent city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> belonged to the German Confederation but not to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zollverein">German Customs Union</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a> it was obliged to join the North German Federation, but Article 34 of the North German constitution kept Hamburg and the other Hanseatic cities as free ports outside the customs border until they applied for inclusion. Article 34 was carried into the imperial constitution of 1871. Hamburg came under great pressure from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> after 1879, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/customs-union">Customs Union</a> greatly increased its external tariff. In 1881 an agreement was signed under which Hamburg joined the Customs Union with all its territory except a permanent free port district, for which Article 34 still applied.</p><p>In 1883 the demolition of the Kehrwieder and Wandrahm area began to clear space, and more than 20,000 people had to be relocated. The Freihafen-Lagerhaus-Gesellschaft, the predecessor of the Hamburger Hafen und Logistik AG, managed the construction, which was completed before the start of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>, and it was also responsible for the later operation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Speicherstadt, meaning City of Warehouses, is the largest warehouse district in the world whose buildings stand on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>-pile foundations, in this case oak logs. It was built from 1883 to 1927 in the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> as a free zone in which goods could be transferred without paying customs. It is 1.5 kilometres long and interlaced by loading canals, called Fleets in Low German. The warehouses were built with different support structures, but Franz Andreas Meyer gave them a Neo-Gothic red-brick outer layer with small towers, alcoves and glazed terracotta ornaments. They are multi-storey buildings with entrances from both water and land, and one of the oldest, Kaispeicher B, houses the International Maritime Museum.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>About half of the buildings were destroyed in the bombing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-gomorrah">Operation Gomorrah</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, and a conservative rebuilding was finished in 1967. The Hanseatic Trade Center occupies the sites of the completely destroyed structures. The district was listed as a protected heritage site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> in 1991 and has been part of the HafenCity quarter since 2008. On 5 July 2015 the Speicherstadt and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kontorhaus-district">Kontorhaus District</a> were made a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, as an exceptional example of Neo-Gothic and modernist architecture and for its testimony to the development of international maritime trade. It is a major tourist attraction, with museums such as the German Customs Museum and Miniatur Wunderland, while some buildings keep their original use as warehouses.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Speicherstadt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the nineteenth and twentieth centuries the port and HAPAG tied Germany to world markets, and the <b>Speicherstadt</b> warehouse district, built from 1883, and the Kontorhaus district with the Chilehaus became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2015.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-hamburg">Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
